/* Bassiet book, Depth 7: The Marrow Forges. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c7-red']={depth:7,title:'The Red Rivers',
epi:'The forges pour out red cells by the billion. Here they come out pale, small, misshapen. Something is starving them.',
c:['anemia','microcytic','b12-folate','g6pd','sickle-cell','o2-hb','bilirubin'],
S:[
{h:'Sorting anaemia by size',fig:'anemia',p:[
'Start with the **MCV**. **Microcytic** (below 80 fL) means the cell could not fill itself with haemoglobin, so it divided once more and came out small. Haemoglobin is haem plus globin, so the causes fall into two families: too little haem (iron deficiency, anaemia of chronic disease, lead poisoning, sideroblastic anaemia) or too little globin (the thalassaemias). **Normocytic** anaemia splits by the **reticulocyte** count: low means the marrow is not answering (chronic disease, kidney disease, aplastic anaemia, early iron deficiency); high means it is answering a loss (bleeding or **haemolysis**). **Macrocytic** (above 100 fL) is megaloblastic (B12 or folate deficiency, drugs that block DNA synthesis) or non-megaloblastic (alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, a high reticulocyte count, since young cells are larger).',
'Iron deficiency develops in a fixed order: stores empty first (**ferritin** falls), then serum iron and transferrin saturation fall while the liver makes more transferrin (**TIBC** rises), and only then do the new cells come out small and pale with a wide spread of sizes (**raised RDW**). A low ferritin is diagnostic, but ferritin is also an acute-phase protein, so inflammation can hide iron deficiency behind a normal value.'],
table:{cols:['Microcytic cause','Mechanism, labs and clues'],rows:[
['Iron deficiency','stores exhausted; ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC, ↓saturation, ↑RDW, pencil cells, reactive thrombocytosis; menstrual or gut blood loss (in a man or an older woman, look for colorectal cancer), hookworm, coeliac disease, pregnancy; Plummer-Vinson syndrome (oesophageal webs, glossitis, koilonychia)'],
['Anaemia of chronic disease','inflammation (IL-6) makes the liver release **hepcidin**, which degrades ferroportin and locks iron inside macrophages and enterocytes; ↓serum iron, ↓TIBC, normal or ↑ferritin, ↓saturation; usually normocytic, microcytic when long-standing; treat the cause'],
['α-thalassaemia','deletions among the four α-globin genes (two on each chromosome 16). One gene lost: silent carrier. Two lost: α-thalassaemia trait, mild microcytosis with normal electrophoresis in adults; the **cis** deletion (--/αα) is commoner in Southeast Asian ancestry and can give a child no α genes at all; the **trans** form (-α/-α) is commoner in African ancestry. Three lost: **HbH disease** (β₄ tetramers, haemolysis, splenomegaly). Four lost: **Hb Barts** (γ₄), hydrops fetalis'],
['β-thalassaemia minor','one β-globin gene (chromosome 11) carries a point mutation; mild microcytic anaemia, target cells, normal iron studies, **↑HbA₂** (above about 3.5%) on electrophoresis; Mentzer index (MCV ÷ RBC count) below 13; no treatment, and iron should not be given'],
['β-thalassaemia major','no working β gene; well at birth (HbF), ill by 6 months; HbF and HbA₂ high, no HbA; ineffective erythropoiesis expands the marrow (crew-cut skull, chipmunk face), extramedullary haematopoiesis, hepatosplenomegaly; lifelong transfusion causes iron overload (chelate with deferoxamine or deferasirox)'],
['Lead poisoning','lead blocks ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase: ↑ALA, ↑zinc protoporphyrin, coarse basophilic stippling; abdominal colic, wrist drop, gum and metaphyseal lead lines, encephalopathy in children; chelate (succimer, EDTA, dimercaprol)'],
['Sideroblastic anaemia','iron reaches the mitochondria but cannot enter haem, so it rings the nucleus (ring sideroblasts); X-linked ALA synthase defect (may respond to pyridoxine), alcohol, isoniazid (B6), copper deficiency, myelodysplasia; ↑ferritin, ↑iron, ↑saturation']]},
s:[['bio','Haem synthesis starts in mitochondria (ALA synthase, rate-limiting, needs B6), moves to the cytoplasm, and ends back in mitochondria (ferrochelatase adds iron). Acute intermittent porphyria (porphobilinogen deaminase): abdominal pain, neuropsychiatric symptoms, port-wine urine, triggered by P450 inducers and fasting; give haemin and glucose, which switch ALA synthase off. Porphyria cutanea tarda (uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase): blistering skin in sun, linked to hepatitis C and alcohol.'],['path','In the thalassaemias the damage comes as much from the surplus chain as from the missing one. Unpaired α chains in β-thalassaemia precipitate inside red cell precursors and kill them in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis); unpaired β chains in HbH disease form soluble tetramers that damage mature cells in the circulation, so HbH is chiefly haemolytic.']]},
{h:'Reading iron studies',p:[
'Four numbers tell the story. **Serum iron** is what is in transit. **TIBC** measures transferrin, which the liver makes more of when it senses low iron and less of in inflammation. **Ferritin** is stored iron (and an acute-phase protein). **Saturation** is iron ÷ TIBC. Read them together: a low iron with a high TIBC means the body is hungry for iron; a low iron with a low TIBC and plenty of ferritin means the iron is there but hepcidin is hiding it.',
'When microcytosis comes with normal iron studies, the defect is in globin, not haem: order **haemoglobin electrophoresis**. A raised HbA₂ confirms β-thalassaemia minor. Normal electrophoresis in an adult with a low MCV and a high red cell count points to α-thalassaemia trait, which needs DNA testing to prove. HbH can be seen as "golf ball" inclusions with a supravital stain.'],
table:{cols:['Condition','Serum iron','TIBC','Ferritin','Saturation'],rows:[
['Iron deficiency','↓','↑','↓','↓↓'],
['Anaemia of chronic disease','↓','↓','normal or ↑','↓ or normal'],
['Thalassaemia trait (α or β)','normal','normal','normal','normal'],
['Sideroblastic anaemia','↑','normal or ↓','↑','↑'],
['Haemochromatosis','↑','↓','↑↑','↑↑ (above 45%)'],
['Pregnancy or oral contraceptive','normal','↑','normal','normal or ↓']]},
key:'Hungry marrow: low ferritin, high TIBC. Hidden iron: high ferritin, low TIBC. Normal iron studies with small cells: check the globin.',
s:[['phys','Iron is absorbed as Fe²⁺ in the duodenum (DMT1), stored as ferritin in the enterocyte, and exported to the blood through ferroportin, then carried by transferrin. There is no regulated way to excrete iron, so the body controls only how much enters: hepcidin is the gatekeeper. Hepcidin is low in hereditary haemochromatosis (HFE) and in ineffective erythropoiesis (β-thalassaemia), which is why both load iron even without transfusion.']]},
{h:'Megaloblastic anaemia',p:[
'**B12 (cobalamin)** and **folate** are both needed to make thymidine for DNA. Deficiency delays nuclear maturation while the cytoplasm keeps growing: large oval red cells, **hypersegmented neutrophils** (6 or more lobes), pancytopenia, and ineffective haematopoiesis (cells die in the marrow, so LDH and unconjugated bilirubin rise).',
'B12 travels a long road. Haptocorrin (R-protein) from saliva protects it through the stomach; pancreatic proteases free it in the duodenum; **intrinsic factor** from gastric parietal cells binds it; and the complex is taken up in the **terminal ileum**. A break anywhere causes deficiency: pernicious anaemia, gastrectomy, pancreatic insufficiency, Crohn disease or ileal resection, bacterial overgrowth, the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum. Stores last years, so deficiency appears slowly.',
'Folate deficiency: poor diet (alcohol), pregnancy, haemolysis (high demand), methotrexate, trimethoprim, phenytoin; it is absorbed in the jejunum and stores last only months. **Homocysteine** raised, **methylmalonic acid normal**. B12 deficiency: **pernicious anaemia** (autoantibodies to intrinsic factor and parietal cells, atrophic gastritis, gastric cancer risk), vegans, terminal ileum disease, metformin, proton pump inhibitors, and nitrous oxide (it oxidises cobalamin, so repeated recreational use can cause sudden cord damage). **Both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid raised**, and **neurological damage**: subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts (lost vibration and position sense, spasticity, ataxia), neuropathy, dementia.'],
key:'Giving folate to a B12-deficient patient corrects the anaemia but not the nerve damage.',
s:[['bio','B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase (homocysteine → methionine, with methyl-THF) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (methylmalonyl-CoA → succinyl-CoA). Odd-chain fatty acids and some amino acids feed into the second reaction, so only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid. Orotic aciduria (UMP synthase defect) gives a megaloblastic anaemia that ignores B12 and folate, without hyperammonaemia; uridine treats it.'],['pharm','Methotrexate blocks human dihydrofolate reductase and trimethoprim the bacterial one (weakly the human one too); folinic acid (leucovorin) is already reduced, so it bypasses the block. Hydroxyurea, zidovudine and azathioprine also give large cells by slowing DNA synthesis.']]},
{h:'Haemolysis',p:[
'Red cells destroyed early raise **LDH**, **unconjugated bilirubin** and the **reticulocyte count**, and lower **haptoglobin**. **Extravascular** haemolysis happens in the spleen and liver, where macrophages eat damaged or antibody-coated cells (splenomegaly, jaundice, pigment gallstones). **Intravascular** haemolysis releases haemoglobin into the plasma: haptoglobin is used up, then haemoglobinuria, and later haemosiderin in the urine.',
'Sort the cause by asking whether the fault is in the cell (membrane, enzyme, haemoglobin) or outside it (antibody, complement, damaged vessels). The **direct Coombs test** detects antibody or complement on the red cell and separates immune from non-immune causes. The film adds the rest: spherocytes (hereditary spherocytosis or warm autoimmune haemolysis), bite cells and Heinz bodies (oxidant injury), sickle cells, schistocytes (fragmentation).'],
table:{cols:['Cause','Mechanism and clues'],rows:[
['Hereditary spherocytosis','ankyrin, spectrin or band 3 defects (usually AD); membrane is lost, cells round up; spherocytes, ↑MCHC, osmotic fragility or EMA binding test, splenomegaly, pigment stones, parvovirus aplastic crisis; splenectomy (after vaccination) for severe disease'],
['G6PD deficiency','X-linked; no NADPH, so glutathione cannot be kept reduced; oxidant stress (fava beans, sulfonamides, primaquine, dapsone, rasburicase, infection) a day or two before; Heinz bodies, bite cells; intravascular and splenic destruction; enzyme levels can read falsely normal during an attack, because young cells have more enzyme'],
['Pyruvate kinase deficiency','autosomal recessive; no ATP from glycolysis, so the membrane pumps fail; rigid cells destroyed in the spleen; ↑2,3-BPG eases oxygen delivery'],
['Sickle cell disease','Glu→Val at β6; HbS polymerises when deoxygenated; vaso-occlusion (dactylitis, acute chest, stroke, priapism, avascular necrosis), autosplenectomy (Howell-Jolly bodies, encapsulated bacteria, Salmonella osteomyelitis), renal papillary necrosis; hydroxyurea raises HbF. Trait: usually well, isosthenuria, renal medullary carcinoma, protection from falciparum malaria'],
['HbC disease','Glu→Lys at β6; target cells, crystals, mild haemolysis'],
['Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria','acquired PIGA mutation, no GPI anchor, so no CD55/CD59 to restrain complement; intravascular lysis, thrombosis in odd places (hepatic, portal, cerebral veins), pancytopenia; flow cytometry; eculizumab or ravulizumab (C5 inhibitors; vaccinate against meningococcus)'],
['Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia','warm (IgG at body temperature, spherocytes, spleen; SLE, CLL, methyldopa; steroids) or cold (IgM fixes complement in the cool periphery, C3b-coated cells cleared in the liver with some intravascular lysis, acrocyanosis; Mycoplasma, EBV, lymphoma); direct Coombs positive'],
['Microangiopathic','schistocytes cut by fibrin strands or abnormal surfaces: TTP, HUS, DIC, HELLP, malignant hypertension, prosthetic valves']]},
s:[['phys','Oxygen dissociation curve shifts right (easier unloading) with ↑CO₂, ↑H⁺ (acid), ↑2,3-BPG, ↑temperature, exercise. Left with HbF, carbon monoxide, methaemoglobin, alkalosis, cold. HbF binds 2,3-BPG poorly, so it holds O₂ more tightly and pulls oxygen across the placenta.'],['bio','The hexose monophosphate shunt is the red cell\'s only source of NADPH; G6PD is its first, rate-limiting enzyme. NADPH keeps glutathione reduced, and reduced glutathione neutralises hydrogen peroxide. Without it, oxidised haemoglobin precipitates as Heinz bodies and splenic macrophages bite them out.']]},
{h:'When the marrow fails',p:[
'**Aplastic anaemia** is pancytopenia with an empty, fatty marrow (the diagnosis needs a biopsy). Causes: idiopathic (T cells attack stem cells), drugs (chloramphenicol, benzene, chemotherapy, carbamazepine, gold), viruses (seronegative hepatitis, EBV, HIV, CMV), inherited marrow failure, radiation. A small PNH clone is often found alongside it. Treat with immunosuppression (antithymocyte globulin, ciclosporin, plus eltrombopag) or a stem cell transplant in the young.',
'**Fanconi anaemia** is an inherited DNA-repair defect: short stature, absent or abnormal thumbs and radii, café-au-lait spots, marrow failure in childhood and a high risk of AML. **Diamond-Blackfan anaemia** affects only red cells in infancy (ribosomal protein genes; triphalangeal thumbs). **Pure red cell aplasia** in adults is linked to thymoma and parvovirus in the immunosuppressed. **Myelophthisis** is marrow replaced by tumour or fibrosis: teardrop cells and a leukoerythroblastic film (nucleated red cells and immature white cells in the blood).',
'**Chronic kidney disease** causes a normocytic anaemia from low erythropoietin; replace iron first, then give erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (target haemoglobin about 100–115 g/L; overshooting raises the risk of stroke and thrombosis).'],
s:[['micro','Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid precursors through the P antigen and halts red cell production for about a week. A healthy person barely notices, but anyone whose red cells live only days (sickle cell disease, spherocytosis) has a sudden aplastic crisis with a reticulocyte count near zero. It also causes hydrops fetalis, slapped-cheek rash in children and arthritis in adults.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A vegan of 15 years has numbness in both feet, loss of vibration sense and an MCV of 112 fL. Which pair of laboratory results is expected?',o:['↑homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid','↓homocysteine, ↑methylmalonic acid','↑homocysteine, ↑methylmalonic acid','Normal homocysteine, ↑methylmalonic acid'],a:2,d:'bio',c:'b12-folate',x:'B12 is the cofactor for both methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so both substrates build up, and the cord damage marks it as B12. A raised homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid is folate deficiency, which spares the nerves.'},
{q:'A man of Mediterranean ancestry develops dark urine and jaundice two days after eating fava beans; the film shows bite cells and Heinz bodies. Which defect explains it?',o:['Low G6PD, so too little NADPH to keep glutathione reduced','Low pyruvate kinase, so too little ATP for membrane pumps','Defective spectrin, so the membrane skeleton loses surface','Absent GPI anchors, so CD55 and CD59 cannot restrain complement'],a:0,d:'bio',c:'g6pd',x:'G6PD makes NADPH in the hexose monophosphate shunt, and NADPH keeps glutathione ready to clear oxidants such as those in fava beans. Pyruvate kinase deficiency also causes haemolysis but is not triggered by oxidants and does not make Heinz bodies.'},
{q:'A man taking isoniazid for latent TB has a microcytic anaemia, ferritin 300 µg/L and ring sideroblasts in the marrow. Which vitamin should be replaced?',o:['Thiamine (B1)','Niacin (B3)','Cobalamin (B12)','Pyridoxine (B6)'],a:3,d:'bio',c:'anemia',x:'Isoniazid depletes pyridoxine, and pyridoxal phosphate is the cofactor for ALA synthase, the first step of haem synthesis; iron reaches the mitochondria but cannot be built into haem. B12 deficiency gives large cells, not ring sideroblasts.'},
{q:'A child with sickle cell disease suddenly becomes very pale and tired. The spleen is not enlarged and the reticulocyte count is near zero. Cause?',o:['Splenic sequestration crisis','Acute chest syndrome','Parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis','Folate deficiency from high turnover'],a:2,d:'micro',c:'sickle-cell',x:'Parvovirus B19 enters erythroid precursors through the P antigen and stops red cell production; cells that live only 10–20 days cannot cover the gap. Splenic sequestration also causes sudden pallor, but the spleen swells and reticulocytes rise.'},
{q:'A woman of Southeast Asian ancestry has Hb 112 g/L, MCV 64 fL, a high red cell count, normal ferritin and normal HbA₂ and HbF. Her partner has the same results. What risk does their pregnancy carry?',o:['β-thalassaemia major from two β-thalassaemia minor parents','Hb Barts hydrops fetalis, if both carry cis α-gene deletions','HbH disease only, as both carry trans α-gene deletions','No inherited risk, as this is iron deficiency in both'],a:1,d:'path',c:'microcytic',x:'Small cells with normal iron and normal electrophoresis suggest α-thalassaemia trait, and in Southeast Asian ancestry it is usually the cis deletion (--/αα), so a child can inherit no α genes and make only γ₄ (Hb Barts). β-thalassaemia minor would show a raised HbA₂, and trans carriers can only pass on one α gene each.'},
{q:'A woman with active rheumatoid arthritis has Hb 104 g/L, MCV 82 fL, low serum iron, low TIBC and ferritin 420 µg/L. What is the main mechanism?',o:['Chronic gastric blood loss from NSAIDs has emptied the iron stores','A β-globin mutation lowers chain production','Inflammation raises hepcidin, which traps iron in macrophages','Lack of B6 stops iron entering protoporphyrin'],a:2,d:'path',c:'microcytic',x:'IL-6 drives hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin so iron is stored but not released: low serum iron, low TIBC, high ferritin. NSAID blood loss is the tempting answer, but true iron deficiency would show a low ferritin and a high TIBC.'},
{q:'A newborn\'s blood holds oxygen more tightly than his mother\'s at the same PO₂. What explains it?',o:['HbF binds 2,3-BPG poorly, shifting its curve to the left','HbF binds 2,3-BPG strongly, shifting its curve to the right','Fetal blood is more alkaline, shifting the curve to the right','HbF has four α chains that bind oxygen more cooperatively'],a:0,d:'phys',c:'o2-hb',x:'The γ chains of HbF (α₂γ₂) bind 2,3-BPG weakly, so its oxygen affinity is higher and the curve sits to the left, which pulls oxygen across the placenta. A right shift would do the opposite and favour unloading.'}
]};

BOOK['c7-clot']={depth:7,title:'The Clotting Gates',
epi:'Gates that should close only at a wound. Here some will not close, and some close everywhere.',
c:['coagulation','platelets','pe','vitamins'],
S:[
{h:'Primary haemostasis: the platelet plug',p:[
'After injury, vessels constrict (endothelin), exposed collagen binds **von Willebrand factor**, and platelets stick through **GpIb**. They change shape and release **ADP** (binds P2Y12) and **thromboxane A₂** (made by COX-1), which recruit more platelets and switch on **GpIIb/IIIa**, which binds fibrinogen to link platelets together. Healthy endothelium keeps all this local by releasing prostacyclin and nitric oxide.',
'Disorders of primary haemostasis cause **mucosal and skin bleeding**: petechiae, epistaxis, gum bleeding, heavy periods, bleeding straight after a cut. The **platelet count** and the **bleeding time** (or PFA-100) are abnormal; PT and PTT are usually normal. Disorders of the cascade bleed differently: deep and delayed, into joints and muscles.',
'The **ristocetin** test separates the adhesion disorders. Ristocetin makes vWF bind GpIb, so platelets clump only if both are present. In von Willebrand disease clumping fails but is restored by adding normal plasma (which brings vWF); in Bernard-Soulier syndrome it fails and normal plasma does not help, because the platelets themselves lack GpIb.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Defect','Clues'],rows:[
['Immune thrombocytopenia','IgG against GpIIb/IIIa; spleen removes platelets','children after a virus (self-limiting), women (chronic); isolated low platelets, megakaryocytes plentiful; steroids, IVIG, TPO agonists, rituximab, splenectomy'],
['TTP','ADAMTS13 deficiency (usually an autoantibody): ultra-large vWF multimers string platelets together in small vessels','fever, thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolysis, kidney injury, neurological signs; PT and PTT normal; plasma exchange, steroids, caplacizumab (anti-vWF), rituximab'],
['HUS','Shiga toxin (EHEC O157:H7) damages endothelium; atypical HUS from complement dysregulation','children after bloody diarrhoea, kidney failure prominent; atypical form: eculizumab'],
['Bernard-Soulier syndrome','GpIb missing (AR)','large platelets, mild thrombocytopenia; no adhesion; ristocetin does not aggregate, and normal plasma does not fix it'],
['Glanzmann thrombasthenia','GpIIb/IIIa missing (AR)','no aggregation to ADP, collagen or adrenaline; ristocetin normal; normal count and size'],
['von Willebrand disease','low or faulty vWF (usually AD; commonest inherited bleeding disorder)','mucosal bleeding, ↑PTT (vWF protects factor VIII); ristocetin aggregation abnormal and corrected by normal plasma; desmopressin releases vWF from Weibel-Palade bodies'],
['Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia','IgG against heparin-PF4 complexes activates platelets','platelets fall by more than half 5–10 days after heparin, with venous or arterial thrombosis; stop all heparin, give argatroban, bivalirudin or fondaparinux; no platelet transfusion; delay warfarin until platelets recover']]},
s:[['pharm','Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1, so the platelet makes no TXA₂ for its 7–10-day life. Clopidogrel and prasugrel are prodrugs that irreversibly block P2Y12 (clopidogrel needs CYP2C19, so poor metabolisers get less effect); ticagrelor blocks it reversibly and can cause breathlessness. Abciximab, eptifibatide and tirofiban block GpIIb/IIIa. Dipyridamole and cilostazol inhibit phosphodiesterase; cilostazol helps claudication but is avoided in heart failure.']]},
{h:'Secondary haemostasis: the coagulation cascade',fig:'coag',p:[
'Tissue factor on damaged cells activates **factor VII** (extrinsic pathway, measured by the **PT/INR**). Contact activation runs **XII → XI → IX → VIII** (intrinsic pathway, measured by the **PTT**). Both converge on **X → prothrombin (II) → thrombin**, which turns fibrinogen into fibrin; **XIII** cross-links it. In the body the tissue-factor route starts the process and thrombin then amplifies it by activating V, VIII and XI. Factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S need **vitamin K** to be γ-carboxylated so they can bind calcium and phospholipid.',
'Natural brakes: **antithrombin** (inhibits thrombin and Xa; boosted by heparin), **protein C and S** (inactivate Va and VIIIa), **tPA** (activates plasmin to dissolve fibrin, leaving D-dimers).',
'A prolonged clotting time is either a missing factor or an inhibitor. A **mixing study** tells them apart: mix the patient\'s plasma with normal plasma. A deficiency corrects, because the normal plasma supplies the factor; an inhibitor (lupus anticoagulant, an acquired factor VIII antibody) does not.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','PT','PTT','Platelets','Notes'],rows:[
['Haemophilia A (VIII) / B (IX)','normal','↑','normal','X-linked; haemarthroses, deep muscle bleeds; factor replacement, emicizumab (bridges IXa and X) in A'],
['Haemophilia C (XI)','normal','↑','normal','autosomal; Ashkenazi ancestry; bleeding after surgery'],
['Vitamin K deficiency / warfarin','↑','↑ (mild)','normal','newborns (give vitamin K at birth), malabsorption, antibiotics; PT rises first because VII has the shortest half-life'],
['Liver disease','↑','↑','↓ (spleen)','all factors fall except VIII (made in endothelium)'],
['von Willebrand disease','normal','↑ or normal','normal','bleeding time ↑'],
['Antiphospholipid antibodies','normal','↑, not corrected by mixing','normal or ↓','lupus anticoagulant prolongs the test tube PTT yet causes thrombosis in the body'],
['DIC','↑','↑','↓','↑D-dimer, ↓fibrinogen, schistocytes; sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, APL, pancreatitis']]},
key:'PT tests VII (extrinsic); PTT tests XII, XI, IX, VIII (intrinsic). Both test the common pathway. Mixing corrects a missing factor, never an inhibitor.'},
{h:'Thrombosis',p:[
'**Virchow\'s triad**: stasis (immobility, AF), endothelial damage, hypercoagulability. Inherited thrombophilias: **factor V Leiden** (commonest; Arg506Gln makes factor V resist cleavage by activated protein C), prothrombin G20210A (more prothrombin made), protein C or S deficiency (warfarin skin necrosis), antithrombin deficiency (heparin works poorly), hyperhomocysteinaemia. Acquired: antiphospholipid syndrome, cancer, pregnancy and oestrogen, nephrotic syndrome (urinary loss of antithrombin), surgery, HIT.',
'**Antiphospholipid syndrome** needs a clinical event (venous or arterial thrombosis, recurrent miscarriage) plus persistent antibodies: lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin or anti-β₂-glycoprotein I. Treat with warfarin; direct oral anticoagulants do worse, especially when all three antibodies are present.',
'**Warfarin skin necrosis**: protein C has a short half-life, so in the first days of warfarin it falls before the clotting factors do. For a short time the patient is more prone to clot, and small skin vessels thrombose. People with protein C deficiency are most at risk; starting with heparin cover prevents it.'],
table:{cols:['Anticoagulant','Mechanism','Monitor / reverse'],rows:[
['Unfractionated heparin','activates antithrombin → inhibits IIa and Xa equally','PTT; protamine; HIT, osteoporosis with long use; safe in pregnancy'],
['Low molecular weight heparin (enoxaparin)','antithrombin → mostly Xa','anti-Xa level; renal dosing; protamine reverses only partly'],
['Fondaparinux','synthetic pentasaccharide: antithrombin → Xa only','renally cleared; no reliable protamine reversal; does not cause HIT'],
['Warfarin','blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), so II, VII, IX, X and C, S are made inactive','INR; vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate; skin necrosis early, teratogen; many CYP2C9 interactions'],
['Dabigatran','direct thrombin inhibitor (oral)','idarucizumab'],
['Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban','direct Xa inhibitors','andexanet alfa (rivaroxaban, apixaban); prothrombin complex concentrate'],
['Argatroban, bivalirudin','direct thrombin inhibitors (IV)','used in HIT; bivalirudin in PCI'],
['Alteplase, tenecteplase','tPA: plasminogen → plasmin','stroke, STEMI, massive PE; bleeding; tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid reverses']]},
s:[['path','Lines of Zahn (alternating pale layers of platelets and fibrin with dark layers of red cells) mark a thrombus formed in flowing blood, not a post-mortem clot.'],['bio','Vitamin K is the cofactor for γ-glutamyl carboxylase; each carboxylation turns vitamin K into its epoxide, and vitamin K epoxide reductase recycles it. Warfarin blocks the recycling. Newborns are deficient because little crosses the placenta, breast milk is poor in it and the sterile gut makes none.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 4-year-old boy has a hot, swollen knee after a minor fall. PTT is prolonged; PT, platelet count and bleeding time are normal. Most likely diagnosis?',o:['Haemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency)','von Willebrand disease (low or faulty vWF)','Vitamin K deficiency (II, VII, IX, X low)','Immune thrombocytopenia (anti-GpIIb/IIIa)'],a:0,d:'path',c:'coagulation',x:'Bleeding into a joint with an isolated long PTT in a boy is the X-linked factor VIII deficiency. von Willebrand disease can lengthen the PTT too, but it bleeds from mucosa and the bleeding time is long.'},
{q:'A woman has fever, confusion, platelets of 18 ×10⁹/L, schistocytes and a rising creatinine. PT and PTT are normal. What is the underlying defect?',o:['Antibody to the heparin–PF4 complex','Shiga toxin damage to glomerular endothelium','Deficient ADAMTS13, leaving ultra-large vWF multimers','Consumption of clotting factors by tissue factor'],a:2,d:'path',c:'platelets',x:'In TTP an autoantibody removes ADAMTS13, so huge vWF strings catch platelets in small vessels and cut red cells into schistocytes. DIC also causes schistocytes, but it consumes clotting factors, so PT and PTT would be prolonged.'},
{q:'On day 7 of heparin after hip surgery, a woman\'s platelets fall from 280 to 95 ×10⁹/L and she develops a calf DVT. Best next step?',o:['Switch to enoxaparin and continue','Increase the heparin dose to treat the clot','Stop heparin and give a platelet transfusion','Stop all heparin and start argatroban'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'coagulation',x:'Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is an IgG attack on heparin–PF4 that activates platelets, so it causes clots rather than bleeding; every heparin must stop and a non-heparin anticoagulant must start. Enoxaparin cross-reacts with the same antibody, and platelet transfusion adds fuel to the clotting.'},
{q:'Three days after starting warfarin without heparin cover, a woman develops painful purple patches on her thighs that turn black. Which deficiency puts people most at risk of this?',o:['Factor V Leiden mutation','Protein C deficiency','Antithrombin deficiency','Prothrombin G20210A'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'coagulation',x:'Protein C has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K-dependent proteins, so warfarin removes it first and the skin vessels thrombose before the clotting factors fall. Antithrombin is not vitamin K-dependent; its deficiency shows up as heparin resistance instead.'},
{q:'A woman with two miscarriages develops a DVT. Her PTT is prolonged and does not correct when her plasma is mixed with normal plasma. Diagnosis?',o:['Symptomatic haemophilia A carrier','von Willebrand disease','Antiphospholipid syndrome','Vitamin K deficiency'],a:2,d:'path',c:'coagulation',x:'Lupus anticoagulant is an antiphospholipid antibody that prolongs the test-tube PTT and resists correction by mixing, yet causes thrombosis and miscarriage in the body. A factor deficiency such as haemophilia would correct on mixing.'},
{q:'A man taking apixaban for atrial fibrillation arrives with an intracranial bleed. Which agent specifically reverses his anticoagulant?',o:['Idarucizumab','Protamine sulfate','Andexanet alfa','Vitamin K'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'coagulation',x:'Andexanet alfa is a decoy factor Xa that soaks up rivaroxaban and apixaban; prothrombin complex concentrate is the alternative. Idarucizumab is an antibody fragment that binds only dabigatran, a thrombin inhibitor.'},
{q:'A boy has lifelong nosebleeds, platelets of 90 ×10⁹/L and very large platelets on the film. Ristocetin fails to aggregate his platelets even after normal plasma is added. Diagnosis?',o:['von Willebrand disease','Glanzmann thrombasthenia','Immune thrombocytopenia','Bernard-Soulier syndrome'],a:3,d:'path',c:'platelets',x:'Bernard-Soulier platelets lack GpIb, the receptor that binds vWF, so they are large, a little low in number and cannot adhere; adding vWF in normal plasma does not help. In von Willebrand disease normal plasma restores ristocetin aggregation, and in Glanzmann the ristocetin test is normal.'}
]};

BOOK['c7-white']={depth:7,title:'The White Tide',
epi:'One white cell decided to copy itself forever. Its copies fill the marrow, the blood, the nodes.',
c:['leukaemia','lymphoma','myeloma','p53-rb','chemo'],
S:[
{h:'Leukaemias',p:[
'**Acute** leukaemias have 20% or more **blasts** in the marrow and present over weeks with marrow failure: anaemia, infection, bleeding, sometimes bone pain and a very high white count of blasts that can sludge in small vessels (leukostasis). **Chronic** leukaemias have mature-looking cells and progress over years; many are found by chance on a blood count.',
'Lineage decides the name. Lymphoid blasts are TdT-positive and carry B (CD19, CD10) or T (CD3) markers; myeloid blasts are myeloperoxidase-positive and may hold **Auer rods**, needle-shaped crystals of fused granules. Flow cytometry, cytogenetics and molecular testing now set both the diagnosis and the treatment.'],
table:{cols:['Leukaemia','Who','Clues'],rows:[
['ALL','children (peak 2–5); Down syndrome','TdT+, CD10+, CD19+ (B-ALL); T-ALL: mediastinal mass in a teenager; spreads to CNS and testes; t(12;21) good, t(9;22) poor prognosis; most children cured'],
['AML','older adults; after alkylating agents, benzene, radiation, myelodysplasia','Auer rods, myeloperoxidase+; acute promyelocytic leukaemia t(15;17) PML-RARA causes DIC, treat with all-trans retinoic acid and arsenic trioxide (watch for differentiation syndrome); acute megakaryoblastic in Down syndrome'],
['CLL / small lymphocytic lymphoma','older adults; commonest leukaemia in Western adults','CD5+, CD20+ (dim), CD23+ B cells, smudge cells, hypogammaglobulinaemia, warm autoimmune haemolysis and ITP; Richter transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma; BTK inhibitors'],
['CML','adults','t(9;22) BCR-ABL (Philadelphia chromosome), a constantly active tyrosine kinase; very high granulocytes at every stage including basophils, splenomegaly; low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase; blast crisis if untreated; imatinib'],
['Hairy cell leukaemia','middle-aged men','B cells with fine projections; CD11c, CD25, CD103, TRAP+, BRAF V600E; pancytopenia, massive splenomegaly, no lymphadenopathy, dry tap (marrow fibrosis); cladribine']]},
s:[['pharm','Tumour lysis syndrome after chemotherapy for bulky leukaemia or lymphoma: ↑K⁺, ↑phosphate, ↑uric acid, ↓Ca²⁺ (phosphate binds it), kidney injury. Prevent with fluids and allopurinol (stops new uric acid) or rasburicase (breaks down uric acid already made; avoid in G6PD deficiency).'],['bio','Imatinib fills the ATP pocket of the BCR-ABL kinase, so it cannot phosphorylate its targets. Resistance usually comes from point mutations in that pocket (T315I resists most drugs except ponatinib).']]},
{h:'Lymphomas',p:[
'**Hodgkin lymphoma** spreads in a contiguous way from node group to node group, often starting in the neck or mediastinum, with **Reed-Sternberg cells** (CD15+, CD30+, "owl-eye" binucleate cells of B-cell origin) in a sea of reactive cells; bimodal age, EBV-linked; B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) worsen prognosis, and alcohol may make nodes ache. Nodular sclerosis is the commonest type; lymphocyte-rich has the best outlook, lymphocyte-depleted the worst. Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma is the odd one out: "popcorn" cells that are CD20+ and CD15− CD30−.',
'**Non-Hodgkin lymphomas** are more often extranodal and non-contiguous, and most are B-cell. Many carry a translocation that moves an oncogene next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer on chromosome 14, so the B cell switches the oncogene on at full strength. Indolent lymphomas (follicular, marginal zone) grow slowly and relapse; aggressive ones (diffuse large B-cell, Burkitt) grow fast but can be cured.'],
table:{cols:['Lymphoma','Clues'],rows:[
['Diffuse large B-cell','commonest NHL; can arise from CLL or follicular lymphoma; aggressive but often curable (rituximab with combination chemotherapy)'],
['Follicular','t(14;18) BCL2 (blocks apoptosis); CD10+; indolent, waxing and waning nodes; older adults'],
['Burkitt','t(8;14) MYC; "starry sky" (macrophages among sheets of blasts), nearly every cell dividing; jaw mass (endemic, EBV) or abdominal mass (sporadic, HIV); very fast-growing, tumour lysis risk'],
['Mantle cell','t(11;14) cyclin D1; CD5+, CD23−; older men; aggressive'],
['Marginal zone (MALT)','chronic inflammation: H. pylori (gastric; may regress with eradication), Sjögren, Hashimoto'],
['Primary CNS lymphoma','HIV or other immunosuppression (EBV); solitary ring-enhancing lesion, distinguish from toxoplasmosis'],
['Adult T-cell','HTLV-1; lytic bone lesions, hypercalcaemia, skin'],
['Mycosis fungoides / Sézary syndrome','CD4+ T cells in skin; cerebriform nuclei, Pautrier microabscesses; Sézary when they reach the blood']]},
s:[['micro','EBV drives Burkitt (endemic), Hodgkin, primary CNS and post-transplant lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; HTLV-1 drives adult T-cell leukaemia; HHV-8 drives primary effusion lymphoma and Kaposi sarcoma; H. pylori drives gastric MALT lymphoma.']]},
{h:'Plasma cells and myeloproliferative disease',p:[
'**Multiple myeloma**: clonal plasma cells make a monoclonal protein (an **M spike**, usually IgG, then IgA, sometimes light chains alone). **CRAB** marks end-organ damage: hyperCalcaemia (IL-6 and RANKL drive osteoclasts), Renal failure (light chain casts), Anaemia, Bone lytic lesions ("punched out", back pain, fractures). Also infections (normal antibodies fall), **rouleaux**, Bence Jones protein in urine, AL amyloidosis. Because osteoblasts are suppressed, the alkaline phosphatase stays normal and bone scans miss the lesions. **MGUS** is an M protein under 30 g/L with under 10% plasma cells and no end-organ damage (about 1% a year progress). **Waldenström macroglobulinaemia**: IgM M spike (MYD88 mutation), hyperviscosity (visual change, headache, bleeding), lymphadenopathy, no lytic lesions.',
'**Myeloproliferative neoplasms** are stem cell clones that overproduce mature cells, mostly with **JAK2 V617F** (CALR and MPL in the rest): **polycythaemia vera** (high red cell mass, low EPO, itching after hot water, erythromelalgia, thrombosis; phlebotomy, hydroxyurea, ruxolitinib), **essential thrombocythaemia** (platelets above 450, clots and paradoxical bleeding), **primary myelofibrosis** (teardrop cells, dry tap, massive splenomegaly from extramedullary haematopoiesis). CML is the BCR-ABL member. **Secondary polycythaemia** has a high EPO: hypoxia (lung disease, altitude, smoking) or an EPO-secreting tumour (renal cell, hepatocellular carcinoma).'],
s:[['path','Langerhans cell histiocytosis: S-100+, CD1a+, langerin (CD207)+ cells with Birbeck granules (tennis racket) on electron microscopy; lytic skull lesions, diabetes insipidus, exophthalmos in children; about half carry BRAF V600E.']]},
{h:'White cell counts to read',p:[
'**Neutrophilia**: bacterial infection, steroids (demargination), stress, smoking. **Left shift**: band forms and earlier cells. **Leukaemoid reaction**: a very high reactive count with a high leukocyte alkaline phosphatase and toxic granulation, unlike CML. **Neutropenia**: chemotherapy, viral infection, drugs (clozapine, carbimazole, sulfasalazine), benign ethnic neutropenia.',
'**Eosinophilia**: the dive log reads **WORMED** — **W**orms (tissue-invasive helminths), **O**verreaction (asthma, allergy, eczema), **R**eaction to a drug (DRESS), **M**alignancy (Hodgkin lymphoma, eosinophilic leukaemia), **E**GPA, **D**eficient cortisol (adrenal insufficiency, since cortisol normally clears eosinophils). **Lymphocytosis**: viral infections, pertussis, CLL. **Lymphopenia**: steroids, HIV, DiGeorge syndrome. **Basophilia**: CML and other myeloproliferative disease. **Monocytosis**: TB, chronic infection, chronic myelomonocytic leukaemia.'],
key:'Leukaemoid reaction: high LAP, reactive. CML: low LAP, BCR-ABL, basophils.',
s:[['phys','Steroids raise neutrophils (demargination from the vessel wall) and lower lymphocytes and eosinophils (apoptosis and redistribution).']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 38-year-old man has bleeding gums, DIC, and blasts packed with Auer rods on the film. Which translocation and which first treatment?',o:['t(9;22); imatinib to block the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase','t(14;18); rituximab for the BCL2 clone','t(15;17); all-trans retinoic acid to mature the blasts','t(8;14); intensive chemotherapy for the MYC clone'],a:2,d:'path',c:'leukaemia',x:'Acute promyelocytic leukaemia carries PML-RARA, which blocks maturation at the promyelocyte; retinoic acid (with arsenic trioxide) lets the cells mature, and the granules\' procoagulants drive the DIC. t(9;22) belongs to CML and poor-risk ALL, not to an Auer-rod leukaemia with DIC.'},
{q:'A 70-year-old has back pain, calcium 3.1 mmol/L, a rising creatinine and rouleaux on the film. Alkaline phosphatase is normal. Most likely?',o:['Multiple myeloma','Waldenström macroglobulinaemia','Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia','Metastatic prostate cancer'],a:0,d:'path',c:'myeloma',x:'Hypercalcaemia, renal failure, anaemia with rouleaux and bone pain are the CRAB features of myeloma, and the normal alkaline phosphatase fits purely lytic lesions with suppressed osteoblasts. Prostate metastases are osteoblastic and raise the alkaline phosphatase.'},
{q:'A man with HIV has a rapidly growing abdominal mass; biopsy shows sheets of dividing blasts with scattered pale macrophages ("starry sky"). Which translocation?',o:['t(14;18), moving BCL2','t(8;14), moving MYC','t(11;14), moving cyclin D1','t(9;22), forming BCR-ABL'],a:1,d:'path',c:'lymphoma',x:'Burkitt lymphoma places MYC beside the heavy chain enhancer, so nearly every cell divides and the macrophages eating dead cells make the starry sky. BCL2 in follicular lymphoma blocks apoptosis instead, giving a slow, indolent tumour.'},
{q:'A man has itching after hot showers, a ruddy face, haematocrit 0.60 and a JAK2 V617F mutation. What is his erythropoietin level, and why?',o:['High, because the clone depends on erythropoietin','Normal, because erythropoietin ignores red cell mass','High, because the clone secretes erythropoietin','Low, because JAK2 signals without it and feedback suppresses it'],a:3,d:'path',c:'myeloma',x:'The mutant JAK2 kinase signals as if erythropoietin were bound, so red cells are made without it and the kidney turns erythropoietin down. A high level would point to secondary polycythaemia from hypoxia or an EPO-secreting tumour.'},
{q:'A 52-year-old man has massive splenomegaly, pancytopenia, no enlarged nodes and a dry tap. B cells with fine surface projections are CD11c+, CD103+ and BRAF V600E-positive. Treatment?',o:['Imatinib','All-trans retinoic acid','Cladribine','Hydroxyurea'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'leukaemia',x:'Hairy cell leukaemia responds very well to the purine analogue cladribine, which is resistant to adenosine deaminase and kills lymphocytes. Imatinib targets BCR-ABL in CML, which has a high white count, not pancytopenia.'},
{q:'A 74-year-old with lymphocytes of 40 ×10⁹/L, smudge cells and CD5+ CD23+ B cells becomes jaundiced with spherocytes and a positive direct Coombs test. Mechanism?',o:['Warm IgG autoantibody, cells cleared by the spleen','Cold IgM autoantibody fixing complement in the cold','Complement lysis of cells lacking CD55 and CD59','Oxidant injury from a drug in G6PD deficiency'],a:0,d:'path',c:'leukaemia',x:'CLL disturbs immune regulation and often brings warm autoimmune haemolysis: IgG-coated cells lose membrane to splenic macrophages and become spherocytes. Cold agglutinins are linked with Mycoplasma and some lymphomas, give acrocyanosis, and coat cells mainly with complement.'},
{q:'Two days after starting chemotherapy for Burkitt lymphoma, a boy has K⁺ 6.6, high phosphate, low calcium and a uric acid that is already very high. Which drug lowers the existing uric acid fastest?',o:['Allopurinol','Febuxostat','Probenecid','Rasburicase'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'gout-purines',x:'Rasburicase is recombinant urate oxidase: it converts uric acid already in the blood into soluble allantoin within hours (but causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency). Allopurinol only stops new uric acid being made, so it cannot clear a high level quickly.'}
]};

BOOK['c7-onc']={depth:7,title:'The Poisoners\' Cure',
epi:'Down here the cure is poison too. It kills whatever divides. Learn its targets, and what it ruins on the way.',
c:['chemo','p53-rb','b12-folate','gout-purines','sex-hormones'],
S:[
{h:'Where in the cycle',p:[
'Cytotoxic drugs exploit the fact that cancer cells divide, so they also strike every normal tissue that divides fast: marrow, gut lining, hair follicles, gonads. Many act at a particular phase. **S phase**: antimetabolites (methotrexate, 5-FU, cytarabine, 6-mercaptopurine, hydroxyurea), etoposide (late S–G₂). **G₂**: bleomycin, etoposide. **M phase**: vinca alkaloids and taxanes. Alkylating agents, platinum compounds and anthracyclines are **cycle non-specific**, so they kill slowly dividing tumours too.',
'Each drug has a signature toxicity beyond the marrow, and that is how the exam asks about them: name the organ and the drug follows. Some have specific rescues: leucovorin for methotrexate, mesna for cyclophosphamide, dexrazoxane for anthracyclines, amifostine for cisplatin.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Key toxicity'],rows:[
['Methotrexate','inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, so no THF for thymidine and purines','myelosuppression (rescued by leucovorin; glucarpidase if the kidneys fail), mucositis, liver fibrosis, pneumonitis, teratogen'],
['5-Fluorouracil (capecitabine is the oral prodrug)','pyrimidine analogue: FdUMP locks thymidylate synthase','myelosuppression (worse with leucovorin, which tightens the lock), mucositis, hand-foot syndrome, coronary spasm; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity'],
['6-Mercaptopurine, azathioprine','purine analogues','myelosuppression; dose down with allopurinol (xanthine oxidase metabolises them); TPMT testing'],
['Cytarabine','pyrimidine analogue (cytosine arabinoside)','AML; pancytopenia, cerebellar toxicity at high doses'],
['Hydroxyurea','inhibits ribonucleotide reductase','sickle cell (↑HbF), myeloproliferative disease; macrocytosis'],
['Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide','alkylating agents (activated in the liver)','haemorrhagic cystitis from acrolein (prevent with mesna and hydration), bladder cancer; ifosfamide also Fanconi syndrome and encephalopathy'],
['Cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin','cross-link DNA','nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity (cisplatin; amifostine, hydration), neuropathy (oxaliplatin: cold-triggered), myelosuppression (carboplatin)'],
['Doxorubicin, daunorubicin','free radicals, intercalate, inhibit topoisomerase II','dilated cardiomyopathy, dose-dependent (dexrazoxane protects); red urine; extravasation injury'],
['Bleomycin','free radicals break DNA','pulmonary fibrosis, skin changes; little marrow toxicity'],
['Vincristine','binds tubulin, blocks microtubule assembly','peripheral neuropathy, constipation and paralytic ileus; little marrow toxicity'],
['Vinblastine','same','myelosuppression'],
['Paclitaxel, docetaxel','stabilise microtubules so the spindle cannot break down','neuropathy, hypersensitivity, myelosuppression'],
['Etoposide; irinotecan, topotecan','topoisomerase II; topoisomerase I','myelosuppression; irinotecan diarrhoea'],
['Busulfan','alkylating','pulmonary fibrosis, hyperpigmentation; used before stem cell transplant'],
['Nitrosoureas (carmustine)','alkylating, cross the blood-brain barrier','brain tumours; CNS toxicity']]},
key:'Heart: doxorubicin, trastuzumab. Lungs: bleomycin, busulfan. Kidneys: cisplatin. Bladder: cyclophosphamide. Nerves: vincristine, cisplatin, taxanes.',
s:[['bio','Thymidylate synthase turns dUMP into dTMP and uses N⁵,N¹⁰-methylene-THF as the carbon donor, leaving dihydrofolate. Methotrexate blocks the recycling of dihydrofolate back to THF; 5-FU (as FdUMP) binds the synthase together with the folate, which is why extra folate (leucovorin) strengthens 5-FU but rescues methotrexate.'],['path','Resistance grows by selection: cells that pump drugs out (P-glycoprotein, MDR1), repair DNA faster or lose p53-driven apoptosis survive and repopulate. Combination regimens use drugs with different targets and toxicities so that no single change rescues the clone.']]},
{h:'Hormonal and targeted therapy',p:[
'**Tamoxifen** (selective oestrogen receptor modulator): blocks the breast receptor but acts as an agonist on endometrium and bone, so it raises the risk of endometrial cancer and venous thrombosis and causes hot flushes. **Raloxifene**: blocks breast and uterus, helps bone; raises venous thrombosis risk and causes hot flushes (as tamoxifen does), but not endometrial cancer. **Aromatase inhibitors** (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) block peripheral oestrogen production in postmenopausal hormone-positive breast cancer; bone loss and joint pain. **Fulvestrant** degrades the oestrogen receptor.',
'Prostate cancer is starved of androgen. **Leuprolide** is a GnRH agonist: given continuously it shuts the pituitary down, but the first days bring a testosterone surge (flare), so an androgen receptor blocker (flutamide, bicalutamide, enzalutamide) covers the start. **Degarelix** is a GnRH antagonist with no flare. **Abiraterone** blocks CYP17 (17α-hydroxylase), so androgens cannot be made anywhere; the build-up of mineralocorticoids needs prednisone alongside.',
'Monoclonal antibodies end in -mab; kinase inhibitors in -nib. **Immune checkpoint inhibitors** release the brakes on T cells, so their side effects are autoimmune: colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, thyroiditis, hypophysitis, type 1 diabetes, rash.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Use'],rows:[
['Trastuzumab','HER2','breast, gastric; cardiotoxicity (reversible, unlike anthracyclines)'],['Rituximab','CD20','B-cell lymphoma, CLL, autoimmune disease; hepatitis B reactivation, PML'],['Bevacizumab','VEGF','colorectal, renal; bleeding, poor wound healing, hypertension, proteinuria, gut perforation'],['Cetuximab, panitumumab','EGFR','RAS wild-type colorectal, head and neck; acne-like rash'],
['Pembrolizumab, nivolumab; atezolizumab','PD-1; PD-L1','melanoma, lung and many other cancers; immune-related toxicity'],['Ipilimumab','CTLA-4','melanoma; immune-related toxicity, often more severe'],['CAR T cells (tisagenlecleucel)','CD19','B-ALL, B-cell lymphoma; cytokine release syndrome (tocilizumab), neurotoxicity'],
['Imatinib','BCR-ABL, KIT','CML, GIST'],['Erlotinib, osimertinib','EGFR kinase','EGFR-mutant lung adenocarcinoma'],['Vemurafenib, dabrafenib','BRAF V600E','melanoma (with a MEK inhibitor)'],['Ibrutinib','Bruton tyrosine kinase','CLL, mantle cell lymphoma; AF, bleeding'],['Bortezomib','proteasome','multiple myeloma; neuropathy'],['Olaparib','PARP','BRCA-mutated breast, ovarian, prostate cancer']]},
s:[['pharm','PARP inhibitors work by synthetic lethality: cells without BRCA cannot repair double-strand breaks by homologous recombination, and when PARP is blocked as well the single-strand breaks they leave turn into double-strand breaks the cell cannot mend.']]},
{h:'Supporting the patient',p:[
'**Nausea**: 5-HT₃ antagonists (ondansetron; QT prolongation, constipation), NK₁ antagonists (aprepitant), dexamethasone, metoclopramide (D₂ antagonist). **Neutropenic fever** (neutrophils below 0.5 ×10⁹/L with fever) is an emergency: start broad-spectrum antibiotics covering Pseudomonas within an hour. **Myelosuppression**: G-CSF (filgrastim). **Hyperuricaemia** from tumour lysis: allopurinol or rasburicase (not in G6PD deficiency). **Mucositis**, **alopecia** and infertility are common; offer sperm or egg preservation before treatment.',
'Two oncological emergencies come from the tumour itself. **Superior vena cava obstruction** (lung cancer, lymphoma) swells the face and arms and distends the neck and chest veins, worse on lying flat or raising the arms. **Spinal cord compression** (prostate, breast, lung, myeloma) starts as back pain, often worse lying down, then weakness, sensory level and sphincter loss; give dexamethasone and get urgent MRI before the deficit becomes permanent.'],
s:[['path','Hypercalcaemia of malignancy is usually PTHrP from squamous cell cancers (lung, head and neck, oesophagus), with a suppressed PTH; direct bone destruction (myeloma, breast metastases) is next, and some lymphomas make calcitriol. Small cell lung cancer causes SIADH and ectopic ACTH. Treat hypercalcaemia with IV fluids, then a bisphosphonate (calcitonin works faster but briefly).'],['micro','Neutropenic fever: Gram-negative rods (Pseudomonas, E. coli, Klebsiella) and Gram-positive cocci from lines are the usual early causes; after a week or more of deep neutropenia, moulds such as Aspergillus become likely (nodules with a halo on CT).']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A woman treated with doxorubicin for breast cancer develops breathlessness, an S3 and an ejection fraction of 30%. Which drug, given with the doxorubicin, could have protected her?',o:['Mesna','Leucovorin (folinic acid)','Dexrazoxane','Amifostine'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'chemo',x:'Dexrazoxane chelates iron and so reduces the free-radical injury that anthracyclines cause in cardiac muscle, a dose-dependent dilated cardiomyopathy. Amifostine is also a protective agent, but it protects the kidney from cisplatin.'},
{q:'A woman receiving cyclophosphamide for lupus nephritis develops painful haematuria. How could this have been prevented?',o:['Mesna and generous hydration','Allopurinol before each dose','Leucovorin 24 hours later','Filgrastim after each cycle'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'chemo',x:'The cyclophosphamide metabolite acrolein reaches the bladder and causes haemorrhagic cystitis; mesna binds acrolein in the urine and hydration dilutes it. Allopurinol prevents uric acid build-up in tumour lysis, which injures the kidney tubules rather than the bladder.'},
{q:'A man with Crohn disease who takes azathioprine is started on allopurinol for gout. Three weeks later he has pancytopenia. Why?',o:['Allopurinol directly suppresses the marrow','Allopurinol induces CYP3A4 and creates a toxic metabolite','Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, which inactivates azathioprine','Allopurinol displaces azathioprine from albumin'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'gout-purines',x:'Xanthine oxidase is one of the two routes that inactivate 6-mercaptopurine (made from azathioprine), so blocking it lets the drug accumulate; the dose must be cut to about a quarter. Low TPMT activity causes the same toxicity through the other route, which is why TPMT is tested first.'},
{q:'A 24-year-old man treated with ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine) for Hodgkin lymphoma develops dry cough, breathlessness and fibrosis on CT. Which drug most likely?',o:['Doxorubicin','Vinblastine','Dacarbazine','Bleomycin'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'chemo',x:'Bleomycin builds up in skin and lung because those tissues have little of the enzyme that inactivates it, so the free-radical injury leads to pulmonary fibrosis. Doxorubicin in the same regimen damages the heart, not the lung.'},
{q:'A postmenopausal woman taking tamoxifen for breast cancer develops vaginal bleeding; biopsy shows endometrial hyperplasia. Which property of the drug explains it?',o:['Agonist at the endometrial oestrogen receptor','Antagonist at the endometrial oestrogen receptor','Inhibitor of aromatase in adipose tissue','Degrader of the oestrogen receptor in all tissues'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'sex-hormones',x:'Tamoxifen is a selective modulator: it blocks the receptor in breast but stimulates it in endometrium and bone, so hyperplasia and cancer of the endometrium can follow. Raloxifene blocks the endometrial receptor, which is why it does not carry this risk.'},
{q:'A child on high-dose methotrexate for ALL receives leucovorin 24 hours later. How does leucovorin protect normal cells?',o:['It reactivates dihydrofolate reductase','It supplies reduced folate beyond the blocked enzyme','It speeds the renal excretion of methotrexate from the body','It inhibits thymidylate synthase in tumour cells'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'b12-folate',x:'Leucovorin (folinic acid) is already a reduced folate, so cells can make thymidine and purines without dihydrofolate reductase, which methotrexate keeps blocked. It does not speed methotrexate clearance; that is what fluids, urine alkalinisation and glucarpidase do.'}
]};
